The Good: BERT is a Master of Emotion
If you look closely at the synopses, BERT has successfully captured the deep, underlying emotional narrative of The Horizon:

The Horizon: A deeply traumatized loner surrounded by death meets a stranger who brings hope and companionship.

Match 1 (Gluttony): A desperate, dying man on a battlefield is approached by a mysterious stranger who offers to change his fate.

Match 2 (Beautiful World): A traumatized loner ostracized by darkness meets a stranger who brings light into her world.

Match 3 (Sita): A tragic story of desperation, death, and doing anything to save a companion from a terrible fate.

BERT doesn't care about the setting. It read the text and mathematically realized, "Ah, this user wants stories about profound trauma, grief, and finding hope through a sudden companion." In purely semantic terms, these are spectacular matches.

The Bad: The Genre Blindspot
While the emotional core is identical, the actual packaging is a disaster.

The Horizon is a bleak, grounded, post-apocalyptic war drama.

The Second Coming of Gluttony is a magical fantasy action series.

My Beautiful World is a college comedy romance.

If a user just finished a depressing war comic, handing them a college romance comedy—even if it features a lonely protagonist—will feel like a broken feature.

Furthermore, look at your similarity scores: 0.9175, 0.9167, 0.9163. The scores are incredibly compressed. In a 768-dimensional space, BERT thinks almost everything is a 90% match because it only focuses on the text structure and vocabulary, ignoring the hard categorical boundaries of the genres.

The Industry Solution: The Hybrid Recommender
Pure text embeddings are never used in isolation in a production environment. To fix this, engineers build a Hybrid Recommender.

We need to force the engine to respect the categorical tags (genres) while keeping the emotional intelligence of BERT. We do this by calculating a secondary score—like Jaccard Similarity—specifically for the genres, and blending it with the Cosine Similarity.

Cosine Similarity (BERT): How similar is the plot/emotion? (0.0 to 1.0)

Jaccard Similarity (Genres): How many exact genres do they share? (0.0 to 1.0)

Final Score = (Cosine * 0.6) + (Jaccard * 0.4)

By applying this penalty, if a Manhwa has a perfect emotional match but has zero overlapping genres, its final score is mathematically crushed, dropping it off the Top 5 list.

